    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p70-q27-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $y=f(x)$ and show that the equation is equivalent to $x^2-xy+1=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p70-q27-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x^2-xy+1=0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p70-q27-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Multiply by ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and rearrange; the steps are reversible because ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x\\ne0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "For $x\\ne0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=x+\\frac{1}{x}\\\\xy&=x^2+1\\\\x^2-xy+1&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nConversely, $x=0$ cannot satisfy the last equation. Division by $x$ therefore recovers $y=x+\\frac{1}{x}$, so the equations are equivalent.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p70-q27-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Use the discriminant to find the range of $f(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p70-q27-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(-\\infty,-2]\\cup[2,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p70-q27-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Require ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\Delta=y^2-4\\ge0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " for real ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Regard $x^2-yx+1=0$ as a quadratic in $x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-y)^2-4(1)(1)\\\\\\Delta&=y^2-4.\\end{align*}$$\nReal roots exist exactly when $\\Delta\\ge0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y^2-4&\\ge0\\\\y^2&\\ge4.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $y\\le-2$ or $y\\ge2$. No root is $x=0$, since the constant term is $1$, so every such value of $y$ is attained. In particular, $f(-1)=-2$ and $f(1)=2$ include both endpoints.\nThe range is $(-\\infty,-2]\\cup[2,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      }
    ]
